<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Callimachus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 310–240 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Kallimachos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Callimachus was a Greek poet, scholar and librarian of the 3rd century BCE, born into a prominent family of Cyrene and active in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> under the Ptolemies. Under the patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-ii-philadelphus">Ptolemy II Philadelphus</a> he worked at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria">Library of Alexandria</a>, where he compiled the Pinakes, a catalogue of all Greek literature in 120 volumes. Of his more than 800 works only a few survive, chief among them the Aetia, six hymns, around 60 epigrams, the Iambs and the Hecale. Favouring short, learned and refined poems over long epic, he became a principal model for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catullus">Catullus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Vergil</a>, Propertius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ovid">Ovid</a>, and through them influenced later Western literature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/callimachus.jpg" alt="Callimachus" width="330" height="489" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Egypt Exploration Society · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:P.Oxy._XI_1362.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Archelaus I was crowned at Olympia and Delphi for chariot racing and received the painter Zeuxis, the architect <b>Callimachus</b>, the poets Choerilus, Timotheus and Agathon, and Euripides.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-macedon">Kingdom of Macedon · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The court also kept scholars and poets: Ptolemy II paid stipends to <b>Callimachus</b> and Theocritus, the mathematician Euclid worked under his patronage, and the chief librarian of the Library of Alexandria was also the crown prince’s tutor.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ptolemaic-egypt">Social Hierarchy of Ptolemaic Egypt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Cyrene, in what is now <a href="/countries/libya">Libya</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=32.82,21.85&amp;year=-265" aria-label="Cyrene on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5110 971 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Callimachus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5560 1241h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5560 1241h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=32.82,21.85&amp;year=-265">What was here in 265 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
